`dns.resolveAny` from `node:dns` does not seem to return anything

## Version

deno 2.9.5 (stable, release, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu)
v8 15.0.245.2-rusty
typescript 6.0.3

## Steps to reproduce

1. Create file `test.js` with the following content:
```js
const dns = require('node:dns');

const host = "www.iana.org";

try {
    dns.resolveAny(host, (err, records) => {
    if (err) 
        console.log({ ok: false, info: err });
    else 
        console.log({ ok: true, info: `records=${records}` });
    });
} 
catch (e) {
    console.log(`Exception thrown: ${e.stack}`);
}
```
2. Run the file like `deno -A test.js`.

## Expected Behaviour
Node.js prints:
```js
{
  ok: true,
  info: 'records=[object Object],[object Object],[object Object],[object Object],[object Object],[object Object],[object Object],[object Object]'
}
```

## Actual Behaviour
Deno prints:
```js
{
  ok: false,
  info: Error: queryAny UNKNOWN www.google.com
      at QueryReqWrap.onresolve [as oncomplete] (ext:deno_node/dns.ts:1:3606)
      at ext:deno_node/internal_binding/cares_wrap.ts:1:3996 {
    errno: -4094,
    code: "UNKNOWN",
    syscall: "queryAny",
    hostname: "www.google.com"
  }
}
```

## Additional Information
The same behaviour was noticed when trying different hostnames.

`resolveAny` documentation states that `DNS server operators may choose not to respond to ANY queries. It may be better to call individual methods like resolve4, resolveMx, and so on. For more details, see RFC 8482.`
It might be that the DNS servers used by Deno do not answer ANY queries.

